WEBVTT

observe
00:00:01.000 --> 00:00:07.000
Observe the pupils. They should be black, round and equal in size.

compare-observation
00:00:07.000 --> 00:00:19.000
There should be no difference in pupil size, no squint and no drooping eyelid.

light
00:00:19.000 --> 00:00:28.000
Switch on the Arclight and select the brightest light setting. Use a quiet, dim room.

distance
00:00:28.000 --> 00:00:34.000
Ask the patient to look at a distant target.

clear-view
00:00:34.000 --> 00:00:46.000
Make sure they do not look at the examiner or the light itself.

move-light
00:00:46.000 --> 00:00:52.000
Move the light from the side towards the pupil.

watch
00:00:52.000 --> 00:00:58.000
Watch the illuminated pupil for the direct light response.

shine-constrict
00:00:58.000 --> 00:01:04.000
Shine the light into the eye and watch the pupil constrict.

compare-responses
00:01:04.000 --> 00:01:10.000
In a healthy eye, the pupil constricts normally.

corresponding-examples
00:01:10.000 --> 00:01:18.000
Reduced or absent pupil constriction may indicate disease of the retina, the optic nerve or both.

pupil-colour
00:01:18.000 --> 00:01:26.000
With a healthy retina and optic nerve, a cataract alone does not weaken the pupil’s response to light.

cataract-constriction
00:01:26.000 --> 00:01:42.000
If the response is reduced in an eye with a cataract, consider coexisting disease of the retina, the optic nerve or both.
